<section id="results">
  <h2>Results and Discussion</h2>

  <p>
    We evaluated the proposed preprocessing and retrieval pipeline on the MIT-BIH ECG dataset.
    All raw signals were successfully converted into fixed-length windows and stored using the
    standardized format described in our pipeline. Each sample corresponds to a 1-second
    heartbeat-centered window (361 samples at 360 Hz), ensuring consistent input across all experiments.
  </p>

  <p>
    Retrieval performance was analyzed using both raw retrieval counts and ranking-based metrics.
    For each query at cutoff <em>k</em>, the retrieved set was evaluated in terms of
    <strong>true positives (TP)</strong>, <strong>false positives (FP)</strong>,
    and <strong>false negatives (FN)</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    From these counts, the following metrics were computed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Precision@k</strong>: fraction of retrieved events that are relevant</li>
    <li><strong>Recall@k</strong>: fraction of relevant events retrieved</li>
    <li><strong>mean Average Precision (mAP)</strong>: overall ranking quality</li>
    <li><strong>nDCG</strong>: ranking quality with position-aware weighting</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Representation Analysis</h3>

  <p>
    We compared time-domain (TD) and frequency-domain (FD) representations derived from the same ECG windows.
    The time-domain representation preserves waveform morphology, including the P wave, QRS complex,
    and T wave, while the frequency-domain representation emphasizes spectral characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Empirically, time-domain representations showed stronger retrieval performance for ECG data.
    This is because ECG similarity is primarily driven by morphological structure rather than
    global frequency content. In contrast, frequency-domain representations were less effective
    in capturing sharp transient features such as the QRS complex.
  </p>

  <h3>Effect of Windowing Strategy</h3>

  <p>
    The use of a 1-second symmetric window centered at the R-peak was found to be critical.
    This window length captures the full beat morphology as well as neighboring temporal context.
    Shorter windows that isolate only the QRS complex resulted in degraded retrieval performance.
  </p>

  <p>
    This suggests that ECG event retrieval benefits from context-aware representations rather
    than strictly localized features.
  </p>

  <h3>Embedding Observations</h3>

  <p>
    Preliminary experiments indicate that even simple representations without complex embedding models
    can produce meaningful clustering of ECG beats. Normal and abnormal beats show separable patterns
    in the embedding space, suggesting that representation quality plays a significant role in retrieval performance.
  </p>

  <h3>Summary of Findings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Time-domain representations outperform frequency-domain representations for ECG retrieval</li>
    <li>Full-beat windowing significantly improves retrieval quality</li>
    <li>ECG similarity is driven by waveform morphology rather than spectral features</li>
    <li>Representation choice has a larger impact than embedding complexity in early experiments</li>
  </ul>

</section>
